ENTERPRISE PRODUCT FOUNDATION

DOCUMENT 09
BACKEND INTEGRATION & API CONTRACT SPECIFICATION

Document Type:
Enterprise Frontend ↔ Backend Integration Contract

Status:
FINAL FOUNDATION SPECIFICATION

Version:
1.0.0

Purpose:
To define the exact and deterministic contract between the
ZyrionOS frontend and backend.

This document establishes:

- API ownership
- endpoint structure
- request contracts
- response contracts
- authentication behavior
- authorization behavior
- loading states
- success states
- error states
- validation behavior
- empty states
- deployment integration
- AI integration
- project/workspace integration
- billing integration
- settings integration
- account integration
- frontend data normalization
- security boundaries
- API failure handling
- retry rules
- caching rules
- real-data requirements

NO MOCK DATA
NO FAKE SUCCESS
NO PLACEHOLDER API BEHAVIOR
NO UI-ONLY STATE PRETENDING TO BE BACKEND STATE


============================================================
01. CORE PRINCIPLE
============================================================

ZyrionOS is a real product.

Therefore:

Frontend must never pretend that an operation succeeded.

If the backend has not confirmed success:

    frontend MUST NOT display success.

Every important state must originate from a real source.

Examples:

Project created
→ backend confirmation required.

Project deployed
→ deployment service/backend confirmation required.

Payment completed
→ payment provider/backend confirmation required.

Subscription active
→ backend subscription state required.

AI generation completed
→ actual AI response required.

User settings saved
→ backend confirmation required.

Workspace files generated
→ real persisted project data required.


============================================================
02. FRONTEND ↔ BACKEND ARCHITECTURE
============================================================

The architecture follows:

USER
  ↓
PAGE
  ↓
COMPONENT
  ↓
SERVICE
  ↓
API CLIENT
  ↓
BACKEND API
  ↓
CONTROLLER
  ↓
SERVICE / BUSINESS LOGIC
  ↓
DATABASE / EXTERNAL PROVIDER


Frontend must NOT directly communicate with databases.

Frontend must NOT contain:

- database credentials
- provider secret keys
- private API keys
- deployment secrets
- payment secrets
- AI provider secrets


============================================================
03. API BASE CONFIGURATION
============================================================

Frontend API access must use one centralized API client.

Example:

VITE_API_URL

Production:

https://api.zyrionos.com

The API base URL must never be duplicated throughout components.

Incorrect:

fetch("https://api.zyrionos.com/api/projects")

Correct:

api.get("/api/projects/me")


============================================================
04. CENTRAL API CLIENT
============================================================

The frontend must have one central API client.

Example:

src/api.js

Responsibilities:

- base URL
- credentials
- headers
- timeout
- request interception
- response interception
- authentication handling
- standardized errors


No page should create its own Axios instance.


============================================================
05. SERVICE LAYER RULE
============================================================

Pages must NOT contain raw API requests.

Incorrect:

api.post("/api/projects/create", data)

inside Workspace.jsx.

Correct:

workspaceService.createProject(data)

Then:

workspaceService
        ↓
api
        ↓
backend


Every major domain must have its own service.

Examples:

authService
workspaceService
aiService
billingService
settingsService
userService
deploymentService
notificationService


============================================================
06. AUTHENTICATION CONTRACT
============================================================

Authentication must be backend-controlled.

Frontend may know:

authenticated
unauthenticated
loading

Frontend must NOT independently invent authentication status.


Required states:

AUTH_LOADING
AUTHENTICATED
UNAUTHENTICATED


When authenticated:

Protected pages become available.

When unauthenticated:

Protected pages must not expose private application data.


============================================================
07. AUTHENTICATION FAILURE
============================================================

HTTP 401 means:

Authentication required.

Frontend behavior:

1. Stop current protected operation.
2. Do not pretend operation succeeded.
3. Preserve useful error information.
4. Allow AuthContext / authentication layer
   to handle session state.
5. Redirect only through the centralized auth mechanism.


Individual services must not implement random redirects.


============================================================
08. AUTHORIZATION CONTRACT
============================================================

HTTP 403 means:

Authenticated user does not have permission.

Frontend must show:

Permission denied

or an appropriate product-specific message.

Frontend must NOT attempt to bypass authorization.


============================================================
09. STANDARD API RESPONSE MODEL
============================================================

Backend responses should follow a predictable structure.

Success example:

{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Project created successfully.",
  "data": {
    ...
  }
}


Failure example:

{
  "success": false,
  "message": "Unable to create project.",
  "code": "PROJECT_CREATE_FAILED"
}


Where pagination is required:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "projects": [],
    "pagination": {
      "page": 1,
      "limit": 20,
      "total": 0,
      "pages": 0
    }
  }
}


The frontend must not depend on inconsistent response shapes.


============================================================
10. PROJECT API CONTRACT
============================================================

Primary domain:

/api/projects


Create:

POST /api/projects/create


Request:

{
  "projectName": "Project Name",
  "description": "Project description",
  "framework": "React"
}


Response:

{
  "success": true,
  "message": "Project created successfully.",
  "data": {
    "project": {
      "id": "...",
      "projectName": "...",
      "description": "...",
      "framework": "React",
      "files": [],
      "deploymentStatus": "not_deployed"
    }
  }
}


Get current user's projects:

GET /api/projects/me


Get project:

GET /api/projects/:projectId


Update:

PUT /api/projects/update/:projectId


Delete:

DELETE /api/projects/delete/:projectId


Deploy:

POST /api/projects/deploy/:projectId


The exact backend route is authoritative.

Frontend must not guess routes.


============================================================
11. PROJECT ID RULE
============================================================

Every project operation must use a valid backend project ID.

Never use:

project name

as a database identity.

Never generate fake IDs in the frontend.

Invalid ID:

→ operation must stop.

Frontend message:

"Project ID is required."


============================================================
12. WORKSPACE CONTRACT
============================================================

Workspace is a real project environment.

Workspace must represent:

Project
Framework
Files
Build state
Deployment state
Live URL
AI generation state


Workspace must never show fake files.

If:

files = []

then UI must display:

"No files generated yet."


It must NOT generate fake:

App.jsx
index.html
package.json

unless those files actually exist in backend data.


============================================================
13. AI GENERATION CONTRACT
============================================================

AI generation endpoint:

POST /api/ai/generate-code


Request:

{
  "prompt": "...",
  "framework": "React"
}


Expected response must contain real generation output.

Possible structure:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "files": [
      {
        "path": "src/App.jsx",
        "content": "..."
      }
    ]
  }
}


Frontend must treat AI output as untrusted external data.

Frontend must not assume:

files exist

unless response confirms them.


============================================================
14. AI GENERATION STATES
============================================================

AI generation has four primary states:

IDLE

BUILDING

SUCCESS

ERROR


IDLE:

"What do you want to build?"


BUILDING:

"Building your project"


SUCCESS:

"Project generated"


ERROR:

"Unable to generate the project."


The UI must never remain permanently stuck in BUILDING.


============================================================
15. DEPLOYMENT CONTRACT
============================================================

Deployment is asynchronous.

Initial state:

not_deployed


Possible states:

not_deployed
building
deploying
deployed
failed


Frontend must display the backend-confirmed state.


Example:

not_deployed
→ Not deployed

building
→ Building

deploying
→ Deploying...

deployed
→ Deployed

failed
→ Deployment failed


============================================================
16. DEPLOYMENT SUCCESS
============================================================

Deployment success requires backend confirmation.

Expected data:

{
  "success": true,
  "data": {
    "project": {},
    "deployment": {
      "status": "deployed",
      "liveUrl": "..."
    }
  }
}


Only after receiving confirmation:

Frontend may show:

"Deployed"

and:

"Open live project"


============================================================
17. DEPLOYMENT FAILURE
============================================================

If deployment fails:

Frontend must:

- stop deployment loading
- display failure state
- preserve project state
- show useful error
- never show live URL unless valid


Incorrect:

Deploy request fails
→ UI says "Deployed"

Correct:

Deploy request fails
→ "Deployment failed"


============================================================
18. BILLING CONTRACT
============================================================

Billing UI must represent real subscription state.

Frontend must never determine:

premium
expert
active subscription

solely from localStorage.

Backend must remain authoritative.


Possible subscription states:

free
trial
active
past_due
cancelled
expired


Frontend displays backend-confirmed state.


============================================================
19. PAYMENT CONTRACT
============================================================

Payment completion must be verified server-side.

Frontend payment redirect/success screen
does NOT equal payment confirmation.

Flow:

USER
 ↓
PAYMENT PROVIDER
 ↓
BACKEND VERIFICATION
 ↓
DATABASE UPDATE
 ↓
FRONTEND REFRESH
 ↓
SUBSCRIPTION ACTIVE


Frontend must not manually mark:

paymentSuccess = true

without backend confirmation.


============================================================
20. SETTINGS CONTRACT
============================================================

Settings page must represent persisted user settings.

Categories may include:

Profile
Account
Security
Notifications
Preferences
Workspace
AI preferences
Appearance


Save operation:

Frontend
→ settingsService
→ backend
→ database
→ confirmation
→ frontend state update


Before backend confirmation:

Do not display:

"Saved"


Use:

"Saving..."


After confirmation:

"Saved"


============================================================
21. FORM VALIDATION
============================================================

Frontend validation improves UX.

Backend validation protects the system.

Both are required.

Frontend:

required fields
format checks
length checks
basic constraints


Backend:

complete validation
authorization
business rules
security validation
database validation


Frontend validation must NEVER replace backend validation.


============================================================
22. LOADING STATES
============================================================

Every asynchronous operation must have a clear loading state.

Examples:

Loading projects
Loading project
Creating project
Generating AI
Deploying
Saving settings
Loading billing
Processing payment


Buttons must prevent duplicate submissions when appropriate.


Example:

Build

becomes:

Building...


Deploy

becomes:

Deploying...


Save

becomes:

Saving...


============================================================
23. ERROR STATES
============================================================

Every API operation must have an error path.

Error UI must be:

clear
professional
non-technical
actionable


Avoid exposing:

stack traces
database errors
internal paths
provider secrets
server internals


Developer logs may contain diagnostic information.

User-facing messages must remain safe.


============================================================
24. NETWORK ERROR
============================================================

If no response is received:

Display:

"Unable to connect to the server.
Please check your internet connection and try again."


Do not show:

undefined
null
AxiosError
ERR_NETWORK


============================================================
25. RATE LIMIT
============================================================

HTTP 429:

Display:

"Too many requests.
Please wait a moment and try again."


Frontend must avoid aggressive automatic retries.


============================================================
26. SERVER ERROR
============================================================

HTTP 500+:

Display:

"The server encountered an error.
Please try again later."


Developer logging may capture technical details.


============================================================
27. RETRY POLICY
============================================================

Automatic retries must be limited.

Safe candidates:

temporary network failures
idempotent GET requests


Avoid automatic retries for:

payments
project creation
deletion
deployment
other non-idempotent operations


A duplicate project must never be created
because of an uncontrolled retry.


============================================================
28. DATA NORMALIZATION
============================================================

Frontend services may normalize backend responses.

Normalization must:

- preserve real data
- handle missing optional fields
- avoid inventing business data
- keep naming predictable


Example:

project._id
project.id

may be normalized internally to:

projectId


But normalization must not fabricate values.


============================================================
29. EMPTY STATES
============================================================

Empty data is a valid state.

Examples:

No projects yet.

No files generated yet.

No deployment yet.

No billing history.

No notifications.

No saved settings.


Empty states must be designed intentionally.

Never use fake records just to make the UI look populated.


============================================================
30. FRONTEND STATE OWNERSHIP
============================================================

Local state:

temporary UI state.

Examples:

activeTab
modalOpen
inputValue
selectedFile


Backend state:

persistent product state.

Examples:

project
subscription
deployment
user profile
saved settings
files


Frontend must not confuse the two.


============================================================
31. CACHE POLICY
============================================================

Persistent backend data should be refreshed after
important mutations.

Example:

Create Project
→ create response
→ update local state
→ optionally refresh projects


Deploy Project
→ deployment response
→ update selected project
→ synchronize project list


Settings Save
→ confirmation
→ synchronize settings state


No stale UI should remain after successful mutations.


============================================================
32. SECURITY CONTRACT
============================================================

Frontend is never a trusted security boundary.

Backend must enforce:

authentication
authorization
ownership
validation
rate limiting
business rules


Frontend hiding a button is NOT security.

Example:

if (!isAdmin) hide Admin button

is UX only.

Backend must still reject:

POST /admin/action

for unauthorized users.


============================================================
33. PROJECT OWNERSHIP
============================================================

Every project request must be authorized against
the authenticated user.

Frontend must never send:

userId

as a substitute for backend ownership verification.

Backend determines ownership from authenticated identity.


